# Which architectures each of robovast's own images is built for.
#
# One source of truth, sourced by container/release_images.sh and read by
# .github/workflows/image.yml, so a local build and a CI build cannot disagree about
# what an image is. Encoding it in both places is how they drift.
#
# The rule is where the image *runs*, not what is convenient to build:
#
#   robovast           developers run this locally (including on arm64 Macs) AND it runs as
#                        a job pod in the cluster -> both architectures.
#   roqsim             amd64 only, for now. Not a statement about where it runs but about
#                        what can be built: its base, and robovast's, is
#                        osrf/ros:<distro>-desktop-full, which upstream publishes for amd64
#                        alone -- the tag is a single manifest, not an index. An arm64 build
#                        therefore takes amd64 content and labels it arm64, which succeeds
#                        (the runner is amd64, so nothing is emulated) and produces an image
#                        that dies with "Invalid ELF image for this architecture" the moment
#                        anything execs inside it. This job is the first thing that does, so
#                        it was the only one failing. That is the same class of silent
#                        mislabelling the CLUSTER_PLATFORM note below warns about, in the
#                        other direction. An arm64 Mac runs the amd64 image under emulation
#                        instead, which works. Widening this again needs an arm64-capable ROS
#                        base, not just a platform added here.
#   controller, sidecar  only ever exist in the cluster, which is always linux/amd64.
#                        The controller used to have a second reason -- the nav extra pulled
#                        PySide6, which ships no linux-aarch64 wheels. That is gone with the
#                        desktop tools, so nothing now *prevents* an arm64 controller; it is
#                        still built amd64-only because that is where it runs, and widening it
#                        should be a change someone makes with a CI build to prove it.
#
# The architecture every cluster runs, and therefore what `--push` must produce
# regardless of the machine doing the pushing: a `docker build` on an arm64 Mac
# otherwise publishes an image no node can run, and the failure is an obscure
# exec-format error in a pod rather than an error at push time.
CLUSTER_PLATFORM=linux/amd64

PLATFORMS_ROBOVAST=linux/amd64,linux/arm64
PLATFORMS_ROQSIM=linux/amd64
PLATFORMS_CONTROLLER=linux/amd64
PLATFORMS_SIDECAR=linux/amd64
